  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Rewrite $6{p}^{2}-12p+7$ by completing the square.\n\n$6{p}^{2}-12p+7=$\n{{field-1}}\n$(p\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$)^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-3}}",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that the leading coefficient is $6.$ So, we take out a factor of $6$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n6({p}^{2}-2p+\\frac{7}{6})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{p}^{2}-2p+\\frac{7}{6}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{p}^{2}-2\\cdot 1p+\\frac{7}{6}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{7}{6}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{p}^{2}-2\\cdot 1p+{1}^{2}}_{\u23df}-\\,{1}^{2}+\\frac{7}{6}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(p-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}+\\frac{7}{6}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(p-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({p}^{2}-2p+\\frac{7}{6}) & =6({(p-1)}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =6{(p-1)}^{2}+6\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =6{(p-1)}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
